 ### 1902 — Mandatory macOS host identity policy fails closed before credentials (TASK-260715-12zaq5)
 - IMPLEMENTATION: The selected libssh2 path now consumes one immutable snapshot-backed `SSHApprovedHostIdentityPolicy`. It accepts only an exact reviewed algorithm plus SHA-256 fingerprint tuple, validates the raw SSH wire key shape, binds the canonical host and port, checks revoked before approved records, and returns typed first-use, changed, revoked, unsupported, host-mismatch, or malformed outcomes before credential lookup.
 - PRIVACY AND ROTATION: Raw host-key bytes remain inside pre-auth policy input and no longer enter accepted session or credential values. First use returns bounded algorithm/fingerprint/canonical-host evidence for an explicit containing-app action; approved/revoked matches return only provenance and first/previous-last/observed timestamps. Printable evidence, decisions, inputs, and acceptance redact host, fingerprint, timestamps, and raw bytes.
-- CONTRACT PRECEDENCE: The task's later binding scope and acceptance criteria explicitly require multiple concurrently approved keys for deterministic rotation, superseding the earlier profile-contract sentence limiting macOS v1 to one active approved record. Snapshot validation now permits multiple unique canonically ordered approved tuples while retaining the eight-record cap and deterministic algorithm/digest ordering.
-- VERIFICATION: Focused Swift Testing covers first use, same/different key, invalid label with identical bytes, malformed key, multiple approvals, revocation, canonical-host/port mismatch, unsupported precedence, production composition, and redaction. A loopback libssh2 test proves all untrusted/rejected outcomes keep credential-provider calls, authentication attempts, and destination channels at zero.
-- VALIDATION ANOMALY: Task-focused suites, strict recursive format lint, core boundaries, native/libssh2 verification, and `swift build` pass. The default full 413-test gate passed once before the final redaction-only contract change, then two reruns failed in untouched HEV UDP tests with the already-recorded association timing flake. A serial full run avoided those HEV failures but exposed two unrelated PacketFlowBridge expectations; `PacketFlowBridgeBoundedTests.reverseTimeBudget` also fails alone (expected yield count 2, observed 1). These exit-2/exit-1 gates remain reported as failing and are not attributed to the host-policy scope.
+- CONTRACT: The independently reviewed `TASK-260715-29ws8l` contract remains authoritative. Snapshot loading and production policy construction enforce at most one active approved identity; deterministic rotation publishes a complete newer snapshot with the former identity retained as an ordered revoked tombstone and one current active approval.
+- VERIFICATION: Focused Swift Testing covers first use, same/different key, invalid label with identical bytes, malformed key, sequential rotation generations, one-active-approval validation, revocation, canonical-host/port mismatch, unsupported precedence, production composition, and redaction. A loopback libssh2 test proves all untrusted/rejected outcomes keep credential-provider calls, authentication attempts, and destination channels at zero.
+- VALIDATION: The corrected loader (13 tests), host-policy (9 tests), and live libssh2 ordering (1 test) regressions pass, as do strict recursive format lint and `swift build`. `make validate-core` first returned 0 with boundaries, native/libssh2 verification, all 413 Swift tests, and build passing. After a test-only expansion of malformed ordering coverage, a second full run passed every host-policy test but hung for more than four minutes in the untouched real-HEV 100-cycle test and was interrupted with exit 130; the final focused suites, lint, diff check, and build returned 0. No HEV code was changed.
 
 ### 1838 — macOS provider resolves exact system-domain Keychain credentials (TASK-260715-1o9wjz)
 - IMPLEMENTATION: The macOS provider now resolves the file-based system-domain Keychain with `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(.system, ...)` and performs one read-only generic-password lookup using an explicit one-Keychain `kSecMatchSearchList`, fixed non-identifying service, exact canonical credential reference, `kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain=false`, and match limit one. The query surface has no hard-coded Keychain path, ambient search, enumeration, fallback, add/update/delete operation, access group, or accessibility attribute.
